# Test: ddos-detect warns when running as an external plugin process
# VALIDATES: plan/learned/1045-plugin-process-boundary.md -- ddos-detect's
# warnIfExternal (internal/plugins/ddos/detect/register.go) logs a warning
# when it runs as a genuine external subprocess, since both its subscribe
# paths (trafficstat.EnsureGlobal/SubscribeRates, and the
# iface.SubscribeCollectNotify fallback when trafficstat is unavailable)
# register a callback into a package-level subscriber list as a same-process
# call that would silently no-op across the process boundary -- the detector
# would never receive a rate signal either way. Warns rather than refuses,
# consistent with ddos-detect's existing trafficstat-unavailable graceful
# degradation framing.
# Uses `ze-test plugin-external ddos-detect`
# (internal/test/cli/cmd_plugin_external.go) to launch the real plugin's own
# RunEngine via a real TLS connect-back, exercising the warning through the
# actual external-plugin transport, not a mocked conn.
# PREVENTS: an operator running `plugin { external ddos-detect { ... } }`
# getting no indication that detection is permanently starved of input.
#
# STATUS: ddos-detect declares Dependencies: []string{"interface"}
# (register.go), so the daemon also starts the interface plugin, which has
# no OS-default backend on darwin ("no backend configured and no OS default
# available") -- the daemon fails before the external ddos-detect subprocess
# ever gets a chance to run. option=needs-linux below; not executable on the
# darwin dev host, but confirmed PASSING under
# `make ze-qemu-needs-linux-test` (real QEMU Linux VM, plan/learned/1045).
#
# // test-relax: replaced the blind 4.0s sleep + wait.py hold with the
# await=stderr fence below. Coverage is REPLACED, not weakened: the
# warn-message assertion (expect=stderr:contains=) is preserved byte-for-byte;
# only the WHEN-to-tear-down changes from a fixed sleep to a deterministic wait
# for the exact line. ddos-detect warns from inside RunEngine after the external
# TLS connect-back and keeps running, so the daemon is torn down by the fence
# rather than by the plugin's own exit; the relayed line proves the warning
# fired through the real external-plugin transport, exactly as before.

option=needs-linux

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
	detect {
		enabled true
	}
}

plugin {
	external ddos-detect {
		run "ze-test plugin-external ddos-detect"
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# // test-relax: the blind sleep + wait.py hold is replaced by the await=stderr
# fence below (see header). The warn-message assertion is preserved byte-for-byte.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
await=stderr:contains=will never receive a rate signal
expect=stderr:contains=will never receive a rate signal
